#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# A paint editor, set up and ready to draw on.
#
#     ./bin/etcs modules/PaintProvider/scripts/paint_editor.etcs
#
# Move the pointer over the window and hold any key to lay down a stroke. The
# window's cross ends the session.
#
# THE SHELL IS THE TOOL PALETTE. There is no on-screen UI here, and that is a
# deliberate reading of what this system already gives you rather than a gap:
# every setting is a verb, so the prompt this drops into IS the settings panel,
# and it is live.
#
#     Root> brush.SetRadius(24.0)                  # a fatter nib
#     Root> brush.SetColor(0.9, 0.2, 0.3, 1.0)     # red
#     Root> input.SetBrush(24.0, 0.9, 0.2, 0.3, 1.0)  # both at once
#     Root> ink.Clear(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)          # wipe the drawing
#     Root> paper.Clear(0.98, 0.97, 0.94, 1.0)     # tint the paper
#     Root> doc.SetActiveLayer(@paper)             # draw on the paper instead
#     Root> canvas.Render()                        # recomposite now
#     Root> input.Report()                         # what the stroke state is
#
# THE STRUCTURE, and the seam between the two providers is one call:
#
#   view    a RenderProvider Surface -- the window's presentable surface
#   paper   a PaintLayer  -- the opaque sheet under everything
#   ink     a PaintLayer  -- what strokes land on
#   doc     a PaintDocument -- the layer stack, and which layer is active
#   canvas  a PaintSurface -- binds the document TO the surface
#   brush   a PaintTool   -- radius and colour
#   input   a PaintInput  -- turns pointer positions and presses into strokes
#
# PaintProvider owns no raster backend at all. It reaches the surface through
# ETCS::resolve_in_family<Surface_>("Surface", rid) -- a composed reference,
# resolved by the loader through the origin-affixed key (core/Entity.h) -- so
# nothing here is compiled against RenderProvider and any other provider's
# Surface would serve just as well.
#
# THREAD LEDGER -- four:
#
#   1. this script's thread   main.ProduceEvents -> input.ConsumeInput
#   2. detached thread        main.ProducePointer -> input.ConsumePointer
#   3. pool worker            Surface.ProduceFrames   frame clock
#   4. detached thread        Surface.ConsumeFrames   present

spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
gpu.Create()

spawn WindowProvider::Window main
main.Create(1280, 800, 'ETCS Paint')

main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
view.Create(@gpu)
view.Clear(0.13, 0.13, 0.15, 1.0)

# ── the document: a sheet, and something to draw on it ───────────────────
#
# Two layers rather than one, because a paint program's first real question is
# "undo the drawing without losing the paper" and a stack answers it by being a
# stack. `ink` starts fully transparent so the paper shows through it.
spawn PaintProvider::PaintLayer paper
paper.Create(1280, 800)
paper.Clear(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintLayer ink
ink.Create(1280, 800)
ink.Clear(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintDocument doc
doc.Create(1280, 800, 'untitled')
doc.AddLayer(@paper)
doc.AddLayer(@ink)
doc.SetActiveLayer(@ink)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintTool brush
brush.SetRadius(6.0)
brush.SetColor(0.10, 0.10, 0.12, 1.0)

spawn PaintProvider::PaintSurface canvas
canvas.Create(@view)
canvas.AttachDocument(@doc)
canvas.Render()

# SetBrush as well as the tool's own setters: PaintInput keeps the stroke
# parameters it stamps with, so a tool changed underneath it would not be seen
# until the next BindTool. Setting both is what makes the two agree at start.
spawn PaintProvider::PaintInput input
input.Create(@doc, @brush, @canvas)
input.SetBrush(6.0, 0.10, 0.10, 0.12, 1.0)

# THE FRAME PUMP, and this script draws nothing without it. A Surface is
# RETAINED, not immediate: Clear/DrawRect/Blit build a composition and
# something has to present it. Without this the window comes up, strokes
# accumulate into the layer, and the screen stays blank -- which reads as
# "painting is broken" rather than as "nobody asked for a frame".
detach RenderProvider/scripts/render_frames.etcs view=view

# Positions on their own thread, presses on this one. An edge blocks the thread
# that states it, and the two channels are independent by construction
# (ontology/InputSource.h) -- a keystroke never queues behind a burst of
# pointer positions.
detach paint_pointer.etcs window=main input=input

# The pump AND the press channel. Returns when the window closes.
main.ProduceEvents() -> input.ConsumeInput()

# The cross was pressed. Close acknowledges it, then the session's entities go
# in the reverse of the order they were built: what draws before what it draws
# through, and the surface before the device it was made from.
main.Close()

input.Delete()
canvas.Delete()
doc.Delete()
ink.Delete()
paper.Delete()
brush.Delete()
view.Delete()
gpu.Delete()
main.Delete()
